WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:06.560
hello and welcome today i wanted to share&nbsp;
with you what in my opinion is the single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:12.880
most useful decorator in python taking a look at&nbsp;
the code that's already written here first we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:18.240
a function which computes the nth fibonacci&nbsp;
number and it does so in a recursive fashion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:24.640
so it's extremely inefficient next in&nbsp;
our main function we have just a loop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.760
going through 400 numbers just printing&nbsp;
out the number and the ith fibonacci number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.080
and then at the end we print done now if i&nbsp;
were to actually run this code you would see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:46.960
that while the first few go very quickly it starts&nbsp;
grinding to a halt around 31 32 33 and essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:53.040
each number after that takes twice as long as&nbsp;
the number before it and this is because of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.880
you know bad implementation of the&nbsp;
fibonacci function that we've given here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:05.520
the main problem with this implementation&nbsp;
is that when i compute fib of n minus 1 well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:10.640
part of that computation is to compute&nbsp;
fib of n minus 2. so i'm doing a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:16.320
repeated work here every level down i'm going&nbsp;
to be repeating work that has already been done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.000
and then just was thrown away in a different&nbsp;
function call an easy way to fix this is just by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.560
remembering values that have already been computed&nbsp;
and that's what the decorator does so my favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:35.920
decorator in python is the cache decorator from&nbsp;
the functools library so functools is a built-in

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:43.920
so you don't have to install anything and&nbsp;
then you just hit your fibonacci function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:47.920
with cache and now let's go&nbsp;
ahead and try running it again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.000
you see it goes through all 400 of them&nbsp;
immediately and the reason for that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:02.080
that even though we have this very inefficient&nbsp;
definition what cache does is it just remembers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:08.240
all of the values of the function that it's&nbsp;
computed before so if you compute fib of 398&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:18.080
then when i compute fib of 399 it looks up fib of&nbsp;
398 and 397 and it says oh i've already computed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:23.440
both of those things instead of recomputing&nbsp;
them using this recursive definition it will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:28.400
just use the value that it's remembered from&nbsp;
the previous time that you've run the function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:35.840
so i find this to be extremely useful because&nbsp;
it allows you to avoid repeated computations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:43.040
there's also a variant of cache called lru&nbsp;
cache lru stands for least recently used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.920
and it does essentially the same thing but&nbsp;
instead of storing all previous values of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:53.760
the function that you've called it with it only&nbsp;
stores a certain number of them so let's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:03:01.680
replace this with an lru cache and give it a&nbsp;
max size of say five this means that it's just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.960
going to store the five most recent values of&nbsp;
the function that i've called now if i'm just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.600
running up values of the function in a for loop&nbsp;
like this then that's plenty just to do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:21.040
so still if i run the function it completes just&nbsp;
as fast as before and this way i'm only using a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.840
little bit more memory but it's a huge speed&nbsp;
up compared to when there was no cache at all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:34.000
so i found myself using cache or lru cache&nbsp;
in a lot of different projects just anywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:39.280
where i can see myself accidentally repeating&nbsp;
computations that might take a long time that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:44.880
a great place to just throw it in a cache or an&nbsp;
lru cache you don't have to do any other work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.520
and it can provide a lot of speed up&nbsp;
if you know the right place to use it

